# eipay, served next to the MoraPay front ends. Imported by the Caddyfile only
# when the deploy script runs with EIPAY=1, which copies this file into the
# runtime sites directory; otherwise there is no such site at all.
#
#   EIPAY_HOSTNAME=eipay.62.238.23.185.sslip.io   (or the real domain later)
#
# Access during integration testing is limited to the named testers: the app
# holds live provider keys (Paycrest has no sandbox), so an open URL would let
# anyone move real money through it before the go-live checklist in
# docs/EIPAY_INTEGRATION.md is met.
#
# How the gate works. A tester signs in once with EIPAY_TEST_USER and the
# password whose hash is EIPAY_TEST_PASSWORD_HASH. That response, and only a
# response to a request that passed the password check, sets an HttpOnly cookie
# carrying EIPAY_TEST_GATE_TOKEN; every later request with that cookie passes.
# Basic authentication alone was not enough: requests the browser does not
# attach cached credentials to — the app's service worker among them — got a
# 401 and the sign-in box came back every few clicks (24 Nov).
#
# One path stays open because the provider calling it cannot log in: the Smile
# ID callback, which checks Smile ID's own signature (and fails closed while
# Smile ID is not configured). The web app manifest is fetched by browsers
# without credentials and is public by nature.
#
# The nTZS webhook stays behind the gate. Tanzania goes through Paycrest during
# testing, and that route accepts unsigned calls whenever NTZS_WEBHOOK_SECRET is
# unset (seen on the server, 24 Nov), so opening it would let anyone post fake
# settlement events. Open it only together with nTZS and its secret.

{$EIPAY_HOSTNAME} {
	import common

	# Not for search engines while it is under test.
	header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow"

	@gated {
		not path /api/kyc/smile-id/callback /manifest.json /manifest.webmanifest
		not header Cookie *eipay_tester={$EIPAY_TEST_GATE_TOKEN}*
	}
	# In a route, directives run in the order written: the cookie is set only
	# after basic_auth has let the request through. A refused request gets a
	# 401 and nothing else.
	route @gated {
		basic_auth {
			{$EIPAY_TEST_USER} {$EIPAY_TEST_PASSWORD_HASH}
		}
		header Set-Cookie "eipay_tester={$EIPAY_TEST_GATE_TOKEN}; Path=/; Max-Age=1209600; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax"
	}

	reverse_proxy eipay:3000
}
